          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="xa14" value="find stuck orders" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="xRecon" target="xPg">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="optTrade" value="&lt;b&gt;4. Key Trade-offs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Write the order to the DB before calling the exchange&lt;/b&gt; vs exchange first (the round's choice). DB-first costs one extra write on the hot path and leaves PENDING rows to sweep; exchange-first can leave a live order at the exchange that the brokerage has no record of. For money, DB-first.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;WebSocket&lt;/b&gt; vs SSE. The symbols on screen change as the user scrolls, so the client must send subscribe / unsubscribe; SSE needs a second HTTP call for that. Cost: WebSocket needs sticky, stateful gateways.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Redis Pub/Sub&lt;/b&gt; vs Kafka for quotes. At-most-once is acceptable because the next tick replaces a lost one and a reconnect starts from a snapshot. Kafka would add durability nobody needs and per-gateway consumer state. Order updates are different: they are also in Postgres, so a lost push is repaired by a GET.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conflate quotes to ~1/s per device&lt;/b&gt; vs deliver every tick. Gives up tick-level fidelity a retail user cannot see; removes 90% or more of the outbound volume.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;PostgreSQL sharded by userId&lt;/b&gt; vs a wide-column store. Orders, fills and positions must change in one transaction; all of a user's rows are on one shard. Cost: a secondary lookup by clientOrderId / exchangeOrderId for the trade feed.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Async cancel (202)&lt;/b&gt; vs synchronous. A fill can race the cancel at the exchange, so only the trade feed knows the outcome." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="3620" width="900" height="400" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="optDeep" value="&lt;b&gt;5. Deep Dives&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;A. Never lose or duplicate an order.&lt;/b&gt; Insert the order as PENDING_SUBMIT with a unique clientOrderId (the Idempotency-Key) and reserve cash or shares in the same transaction. Then submit to the exchange with that clientOrderId. Then update to SUBMITTED. Crash after the exchange accepted: the row is still PENDING, and the fill on the trade feed carries clientOrderId, so it still maps. Timeout: return 202 PENDING_SUBMIT; a retry with the same key returns the same order, and the exchange rejects a duplicate clientOrderId. The reconciler resolves PENDING rows older than a few seconds.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;B. Trade feed correctness.&lt;/b&gt; Dedupe on execId; process one order's events in order (partition by orderId); apply fill, order and position in one transaction. On a feed disconnect, replay from the last sequence number; end-of-day reconciliation against the exchange.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;C. The 1B/s fan-out is the scale break.&lt;/b&gt; 5M devices x 20 symbols x 10 ticks/s = 1B messages/s, 200/s per phone. Nobody reads 200 prices a second. Keep only the latest price per symbol and send each device one batched frame per second: 5M frames/s, about 50k/s per gateway across 100 gateways. Subscribe only to symbols on screen.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;D. Hot symbol.&lt;/b&gt; 5M watchers means every gateway subscribes to it; each gets 10 ticks/s and fans out locally. The cost is per-gateway CPU, not Redis. Conflation caps it at one write per device per second.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;E. Price history off the hot path.&lt;/b&gt; Writing Cassandra before publishing puts a DB write inside the 1 s budget and makes a DB slowdown a pricing outage. Publish first, persist asynchronously in batches.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;F. Market open.&lt;/b&gt; 10x orders and 5M reconnects in the same minute. Pre-scale gateways before the open, jitter client reconnects, rate-limit per user, shed quote frequency before shedding orders.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;G. Monitoring.&lt;/b&gt; Age of the oldest PENDING_SUBMIT order · quote staleness per symbol (now minus last tick) · gateway send-queue depth · trade feed sequence gaps · fills for unknown orders (should be zero)." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="980" y="3620" width="900" height="520" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
